<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Pharsalus</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Pharsalus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Thessalian city of Phthiotis, long ruled by aristocratic families, whose plain saw Caesar's victory over Pompey in 48 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pharsalus stood on a hill above the plain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enipeus-thessaly">Enipeus</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, in the region Greek myth linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phthia">Phthia</a>. It became a city in the Archaic period and one of the leading members of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thessalian-league">Thessalian League</a> with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/larissa">Larissa</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyranny-of-pherae">Pherae</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pharsalus was dominated by aristocratic families such as the Echecratidae. In the 370s BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polydamas-of-pharsalus">Polydamas</a> governed the city and was forced to submit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason-of-pherae">Jason of Pherae</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daochus-ii">Daochus</a>, a Pharsalian, served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> as tetrarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and set up a family monument at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delphi">Delphi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Pharsalus passed under Macedonian rule, was freed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 196 BCE and came under Roman control by 146 BCE. In 48 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> nearby at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pharsalus">Battle of Pharsalus</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:45.77%;width:11.02%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pharsalus</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Polydamas, Daochus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Thessalian Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pharsalos, Farsala</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-423&amp;focus=pharsalus" aria-label="Pharsalus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5222 780 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pharsalus lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5553 1014h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5542 1036h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5542 1036h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Greece. <a href="/atlas?year=-423&amp;focus=pharsalus">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

